  "summary": "Major healthcare policy developments are taking shape as the new congressional session approaches. Big pharma companies are signaling a pullback from certain Chinese hospital trials amid geopolitical tensions, while lawmakers are pushing for stronger U.S. biotech competitiveness against China. The incoming Congress also faces pressure on infectious disease preparedness, with calls to restore measles elimination status after the U.S. lost that designation.\n\nPrior authorization and AI decision-making in healthcare remain contentious issues, with industry panels debating whether insurers, physicians, or AI systems should make final medical-necessity determinations. Meanwhile, the telehealth sector is rapidly expanding into peptides and specialized care, with Hims and Noom launching new programs following regulatory changes, and AI agents are being tested to streamline prior authorization processes themselves.\n\nDrug development momentum is shifting with high-profile collaborations emerging, including Merck partnering with Isomorphic Labs on AI-driven oncology drug discovery, while Sanofi won FDA approval for a rare neurological disease treatment. On the clinical evidence front, researchers are raising cautions about combining vaccines with monoclonal antibodies for malaria protection, and there's emerging debate over deprescribing practices for statins based on new trial data.",
